# **Append-only prose, merged by keeping BOTH sides.**
#
# These two files accrete one entry at the end per branch, so two
# branches working in parallel always conflict at the same place even
# when they touched nothing else in common - on 2026-08-01 that cost
# three merge-and-re-run cycles in one afternoon. `union` resolves an
# overlapping append by keeping every line from both sides, in order,
# with no markers, which for an append-only file is exactly the right
# answer.
#
# Verified rather than assumed, on a scratch repository: two branches
# each appending one entry merge with exit 0 and zero conflict markers,
# both entries present. The one cosmetic artifact is that the blank line
# between them can be swallowed, leaving two headings adjacent - which
# CommonMark renders correctly, so it is untidy rather than wrong.
#
# The cost, stated so it is not a surprise: if two branches edit the
# SAME existing lines, union keeps both versions instead of raising a
# conflict. Both files are append-only by rule, and
# `check-doc-ids.py` catches the visible symptom (an id appearing
# twice), but an edit to an old entry still wants a careful read of the
# merge.
docs/lessons-learned.md merge=union
docs/alpha-feel-notes.md merge=union
